      {
        "id": "def-qc-dyadic-cyclotomic-ring",
        "deps": [
          "def-number-field",
          "thm-cyclotomic-ring-of-integers"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-qc-dyadic-coefficient-encoding"
        ],
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Dyadic cyclotomic ring for Clifford+T synthesis",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "statement": "Set $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/4}$, $K=\\mathbb Q(\\omega)$, $O=\\mathbb Z[\\omega]$, and\n$D=O[1/2]=\\mathbb Z[\\omega,1/2]$. The relation $\\omega^4=-1$ reduces every\nelement of $O$ to four integer coefficients in the power basis\n$1,\\omega,\\omega^2,\\omega^3$. The canonical dyadic encoding of a nonzero\n$z\\in D$ is\n\n$$z=(a_0+a_1\\omega+a_2\\omega^2+a_3\\omega^3)/2^r,$$\n\nwhere $r\\ge0$ is least and, if $r>0$, the four integers are not all even.\nZero is encoded with $r=0$ and all four coefficients zero. Arithmetic is exact:\nmultiply polynomials, reduce with $\\omega^4=-1$, combine denominators, and\nremove the common power of $2$ from the coefficients. The integral-basis\nsupplier [[thm-cyclotomic-ring-of-integers]] and the proof of\n[[lem-qc-dyadic-coefficient-encoding]] justify existence and uniqueness."
      },
